WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.800
EDHRec is a tool that collects deck data from&nbsp;
across the internet, and which creates pages for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:09.360
each commander that contain the cards commonly&nbsp;
played in them. I have spoken before about how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:13.680
using EDHRec for all of your deckbuilding needs&nbsp;
can lead to decks that are flat, formulaic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:18.960
and missing lots of great tools, and I’ve also&nbsp;
spoken about how lots of people building decks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:24.720
mostly with EDHRec causes a handful of unintended&nbsp;
side effects for the broader commander ecosystem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:30.000
These things are true and I stand by them, but&nbsp;
I’ve also seen some players take things too far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.120
in the opposite direction, decrying EDHRec as a&nbsp;
useless tool for anybody but the least creative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:40.480
deckbuilders, and giving themselves a hearty pat&nbsp;
of the back staying away from it. In reality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:45.520
EDHRec is an intensely useful website that&nbsp;
has the capability to make your decks better,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:51.120
more interesting, even more unique in some&nbsp;
ways—if you know how to use it correctly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:57.200
So in this video I want to dial in on EDHREC&nbsp;
as a tool, and talk about how I use it:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:01.040
when during deckbuilding I use it,&nbsp;
what specific features I like to use,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:05.360
and things I like to be careful of&nbsp;
to prevent unintended consequences.

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:10.800
To start with, there are two main times when I&nbsp;
use EDHRec during deckbuilding—very early in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:15.600
process of building a deck, and then sporadically&nbsp;
closer to the end of deckbuilding. These two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:20.640
timeframes serve different purposes—early in&nbsp;
the process of deckbuilding, I want to get a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:26.560
lay of the land. EDHRec is useful as a shortcut&nbsp;
for seeing the most popular, and often the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:31.440
powerful, stuff you can do with a commander.&nbsp;
In some cases these will be the flashiest plays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:35.680
you can do with a given commander—we can see&nbsp;
Fists of Flame at the top of the Zada page,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:40.960
Curiosity at the top of the Niv-Mizzet Parun page,&nbsp;
and Parhelion II at the top of the Greasefang&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:45.840
page. Even if you don’t intend to use the&nbsp;
stuff at the top of the EDHRec page, it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:50.800
good to know what’s there—if you’re looking to&nbsp;
build a deck around Animar, a popular commander,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:55.520
it’s good to know that more than half of Animar&nbsp;
players are playing him as some form of combo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:59.840
deck, because that will impact how your deck is&nbsp;
perceived and what other players will be on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:05.360
lookout for. In other cases, the top of an EDHRec&nbsp;
page might be more like the points of agreement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:09.840
between different builds of a commander. For&nbsp;
something like Atraxa Grand Unifier we see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:14.400
some popular removal spells and some flicker&nbsp;
cards to repeat the commander’s powerful ETB,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:19.520
but after that the play-rates of different cards&nbsp;
fall off pretty fast, with players choosing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:24.480
variety of different approaches for the specifics&nbsp;
of their deck. In this case, I could go “okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:28.560
I guess I’ll start with flicker” and then&nbsp;
decide the specifics of how I want to do that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:35.280
and if I want to be among the roughly 25% of cEDH&nbsp;
players playing Atraxa, judging by this page.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:40.000
Typically I’ll look at an EDHRec page at first&nbsp;
to see any super obvious build paths that other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:43.920
people have been doing, but then once I’ve&nbsp;
decided to build a particular commander I’ll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:48.320
click away for a while. It’s useful to know&nbsp;
the basics of what other people are doing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:52.720
but the longer you spend on this unaltered&nbsp;
EDHRec page without looking elsewhere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:57.280
the more you’ll get sucked into the stuff on&nbsp;
this page and subconsciously let it decide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:02.080
what your deck wants to be for you. In cases&nbsp;
when I’m using a top-down approach to build a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:07.440
deck—basically finding a commander I like with a&nbsp;
card or two in mind—I like to get out of EDHRec&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:10.960
pretty early so that the foundations of the&nbsp;
deck aren’t too heavily influenced by what’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:15.840
popular. In the early stages of building a&nbsp;
deck, I don’t really want to know that 47%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:20.000
of other Imskir players are using Deadly&nbsp;
Dispute, because that colors my opinion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:24.160
of the card at a stage in deckbuilding when I&nbsp;
want to be exploring possibilities and keeping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:28.240
an open mind to different options with the&nbsp;
commander. Other players might be different,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:32.320
but I find that the progression of gradually&nbsp;
building up in cards and plugging in different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:37.040
scryfall searches as I figure out different&nbsp;
ways my deck could play is really valuable as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:42.480
a creative step—it’s a stage of deckbuilding&nbsp;
that I want to preserve as much as possible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:46.800
Regardless of your exact preferences, if you’re&nbsp;
starting your deckbuilding journey in EDHRec,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:52.560
I strongly recommend you click away to futz around&nbsp;
with your own thing for a while at some point.

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:57.680
For me, EDHRec comes back into the picture a bit&nbsp;
later once I’ve discovered a fair number of cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:02.560
and have a decently strong concept of my deck,&nbsp;
but haven’t fully ironed out an initial draft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:07.440
yet. Whereas the initial usage of EDHRec was to&nbsp;
get a lay of the land and see a handful of basic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:14.080
build paths, in this later stage of EDHRec usage&nbsp;
I am looking for specific cards. Scryfall is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:18.960
lovely tool, but there are some things it just&nbsp;
won’t hit in any reasonable number of searches.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:22.720
Dour-port Mage won’t show up in any common&nbsp;
searches you might make for a flicker deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:26.640
and if you’re building a Karametra deck,&nbsp;
self-bouncers like Whitemain Lion might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:31.920
not even enter your radar if you’re not already&nbsp;
in the know. This is where EDHRec is helpful—it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:36.400
can help you fill in the gaps with popular cards&nbsp;
that scryfall searches might have missed. Even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:41.120
if a particular card isn’t showing up on the&nbsp;
EDHRec page due to not being popular enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:46.560
looking at EDHRec can still help you find that&nbsp;
card. If you’re building a cast-from-exile deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:50.400
you’ll probably find a bunch of payoffs for&nbsp;
casting things from exile from scryfall searches,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:56.480
but a brief trip to the Prosper EDHREC page&nbsp;
might clue you in on the words “from anywhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.480 --> 00:05:00.640
other than your hand.” Suddenly, that’s&nbsp;
a whole new avenue to take a stroll down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:05.280
and you might find yourself a lovely Memory&nbsp;
Worm—get in here, you funky little guy.

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:10.160
Okay, so at this point you hopefully have a good&nbsp;
idea of when I like to use EDHRec and why I use it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:14.880
at those times. Deckbuilding is a very personal&nbsp;
process and you may have your own preferences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:18.880
but ideally you’ll be starting to get a better&nbsp;
sense of what role the website should have in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:24.400
your deckbuilding. However, in covering the when&nbsp;
and the why, I’ve so far skipped over the “how”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:28.240
arguably the most important part. This&nbsp;
might be confusing to a lot of people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:33.840
because on a basic level EDHRec is very easy to&nbsp;
use—you pop open the website, find your commander,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:39.680
and you’re off to the races. Easy as cake, right?&nbsp;
Wrong! This page sucks if you’re looking for card&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:43.600
choices for your specific deck, because&nbsp;
it includes every deck for a commander,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:48.480
even plenty that look nothing like the one you’re&nbsp;
trying to build. I think we as EDH players have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:53.440
a tendency to treat commanders as whole entities,&nbsp;
like characters selected from a grid in a fighting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.440 --> 00:06:00.720
game. This game is Kardur vs Zur vs Syr Konrad vs&nbsp;
one of the 63 Kellens—what a legendary matchup!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:05.040
Wanting to simplify this one thing is an&nbsp;
understandable impulse given the intense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:10.080
complexity of the commander format, but it’s&nbsp;
sadly an incorrect impulse. Decks with a given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:15.360
commander can vary dramatically one another, and&nbsp;
the more diverse a given commander’s decks are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:20.320
the less helpful the base EDHRec&nbsp;
page will be. So what’s the solution?

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:24.640
The answer is filters, which I make very&nbsp;
heavy use of once I’m further along and in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:29.920
that card-gleaning stage of EDHRec usage. By&nbsp;
default, the website will draw data from all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.920 --> 00:06:34.480
decks in its database with a given commander,&nbsp;
but the filters option allows you to filter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:39.440
out decks that do or don’t include a given card.&nbsp;
Now, you could put all sorts of stuff in this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:44.080
but the most useful type of card will be what&nbsp;
I like to call “indicator cards,” which are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:49.440
basically just cards that fit very cleanly in&nbsp;
or out of a given archetype. For example, if we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:54.400
go to the page of Kellan, Inquisitive Prodigy, we&nbsp;
see a melange of different elements, but the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:59.040
prominent ones are things involving clues, which&nbsp;
kinda makes sense given that blue green clues is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:03.040
an established Innistrad archetype. This&nbsp;
is going to be helpful to some people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:08.320
but what if I’m looking for a higher-curved deck,&nbsp;
maybe even consider the inclusion of Keruga as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:13.520
companion? In that case this page won’t do much&nbsp;
for me before a bit of filtering, and the one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:18.480
card near the top of the page that really stands&nbsp;
out as a big-mana option is Map the Frontier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:23.920
a four mana sorcery that searches up two basic&nbsp;
lands and/or deserts to the field. And actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:28.320
this works great as an indicator card because&nbsp;
it’s not the sort of card that most decks will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:34.240
want. Mana ramp past two, maybe three mana stuff&nbsp;
will typically only be included in a deck if it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:39.280
fits with a deck’s synergies, so its presence&nbsp;
will generally be a good sign that a deck has a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:44.320
big mana gameplan. Sure enough, that’s reflected&nbsp;
after filtering—we see lots of higher-cost cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:49.200
that would require mana investment to succeed,&nbsp;
as well as lots of great tools for a Keruga deck.

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:54.720
So that’s one example, but what about an example&nbsp;
of an anti-indicator card? A while back I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.720 --> 00:08:00.080
brewing a Kardur control deck, sort of like a red&nbsp;
flicker deck. A major goal of the deck is to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:05.360
a basically unlimited supply of Disrupt Decorums&nbsp;
available, since Kardur has that effect on ETB,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:10.240
and ETBs can be repeated with a card like Jaxis&nbsp;
that can produce a token copy of a creature each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.240 --> 00:08:15.360
turn. I thought this was a sensible enough plan&nbsp;
for the commander, but when I went to the EDHRec&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:21.120
page, a number of the more commonly played cards&nbsp;
themselves did goad-related things. Initially I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:25.040
was pretty shocked that around half of all Kardur&nbsp;
players felt the need to run cards that do the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:29.840
same thing as their commander, but that’s their&nbsp;
business, not mine. The important thing is what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:34.400
happened next: I filtered the results. One option&nbsp;
for this could be to filter by cards I know I want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:39.840
in the deck, such as a couple red flicker staples&nbsp;
like Jaxis and Conjurer’s Closet. However, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:45.360
result is pretty muddy, with Disrupt Decorum now&nbsp;
showing up in an even greater proportion of decks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:50.240
The issue is that these red flicker cards don’t&nbsp;
do anything to indicate a player who is averse to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:56.400
redundancy—there are plenty of goadmaxxers out&nbsp;
there who will take the flicker stuff and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.400 --> 00:09:01.920
additional copies of goad cards, because they just&nbsp;
like disrupting decorum that much. No, the way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:06.560
start sculpting a helpful EDHRec page in this case&nbsp;
is to exclude the cards that do the same thing as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:12.000
the commander, such as Disrupt Decorum and Geode&nbsp;
Rager, and after that the page suddenly starts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:16.160
looking a lot more helpful. There’s still some&nbsp;
of this that’s probably outside what my deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.160 --> 00:09:21.040
would want to do, but at least the goad stuff&nbsp;
is mostly out of the way—Vengeful Ancestor is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:26.560
only in 12% of the remaining decks after this&nbsp;
filtering, down from 43% on the base page.

00:09:26.560 --> 00:09:31.680
As a final example of filtering, let’s talk about&nbsp;
how to combine includes and excludes. Back when I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:36.640
had a Liesa hatebears deck, I’d periodically check&nbsp;
EDHRec to see if anything was catching my eye,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:41.120
but for this to work I needed to filter&nbsp;
the page first. The most popular build of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:45.920
Liesa is lifegain, and though I don’t mind&nbsp;
a little lifegain to offset Liesa’s drain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:51.280
it’s definitely not what the deck is built around.&nbsp;
A first step could be excluding some core lifegain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.280 --> 00:09:56.400
cards such as Soul Warden, but this isn’t all that&nbsp;
effective because lifegain is just such a dominant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.400 --> 00:10:00.240
archetype, and there are plenty of lifegain&nbsp;
decks not including a certain lifegain card&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:04.960
for one reason or another. A different approach&nbsp;
is to filter for inclusion of 2 mana Thalia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:11.360
which is basically the first card I’d add to any&nbsp;
hatebears deck, and this is a bit more effective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:17.440
but lifegain cards still start creeping in&nbsp;
at around 25% inclusion. What works best is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:22.640
the combined approach; if I filter for decks&nbsp;
that include Thalia and exclude Soul Warden,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.640 --> 00:10:28.000
lifegain is basically obliterated from the page,&nbsp;
with a Well of Lost Dreams and two Sanguine Bond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:34.800
effects being the only lifegain payoffs remaining.&nbsp;
This also still leaves a healthy 550 decks, since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:39.360
I didn’t need to throw a whole bunch of cards into&nbsp;
my filters in order to get the desired results.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:42.240
You can do something similar with&nbsp;
price if you’re looking for a budget&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:46.880
deck—if a base EDHREC page is helpful&nbsp;
aside from the cards being too pricy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:50.320
you could exclude an expensive card&nbsp;
and include something cheap like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:55.200
solid budget land. Suddenly, the cards on&nbsp;
the page will be much more in your budget.

00:10:55.200 --> 00:10:59.120
It becomes more clear just how important&nbsp;
filtering is when you think about an EDHRec&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:04.160
page as a cross section of a handful of different&nbsp;
overlapping decks. Some commanders will be pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:09.200
straightforward and less diverse—if we go to&nbsp;
the Prosper EDHRec page, there are almost 30&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:14.240
nonland cards that show up in more than 50%&nbsp;
of Prosper decks. Meanwhile, if we look at a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:20.080
similarly popular but more open-ended commander,&nbsp;
Alela Artful Provateur, that number is less than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.080 --> 00:11:25.360
half of that, and some commanders like Glissa&nbsp;
Sunslayer have almost no cards above the 50%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.360 --> 00:11:31.200
played threshold. For commanders with a singular,&nbsp;
more streamlined playpath, the base EDHRec page&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:35.760
can be moderately helpful, but for a more&nbsp;
diverse commander the page might be pure soup,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:40.400
an unfiltered mishmash of different archetypes&nbsp;
interspersed across the page. I actually made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.400 --> 00:11:45.360
a video last year about exactly these sorts of&nbsp;
commanders, with Archelos, Francisco/Malcolm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.360 --> 00:11:50.080
and Skeleton Ship as examples—these EDHREC pages&nbsp;
of these commanders are equal parts interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.080 --> 00:11:55.360
to dig into and instructive for seeing the&nbsp;
limits of an unfiltered EDHREC page. I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.360 --> 00:12:01.040
also very interested in what causes a commander’s&nbsp;
EDHREC page to be more diverse or less diverse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.040 --> 00:12:05.600
so I actually ended up working with my brother&nbsp;
to analyze a whole bunch of EDHREC pages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.600 --> 00:12:10.400
and we made a video about it over on my second&nbsp;
channel, complete with a tool to analyze your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.400 --> 00:12:14.560
own commanders. I’ll include a preview of&nbsp;
that at the end of this video. Regardless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.560 --> 00:12:17.680
of what exact commanders you’re using,&nbsp;
filters are an important thing to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.680 --> 00:12:22.640
in your repertoire if you’re using EDHRec.&nbsp;
The website as a whole is a very useful tool,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.640 --> 00:12:27.360
both for getting a sense of the broader play-rates&nbsp;
of a commander and for finding specific cards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.360 --> 00:12:31.840
but if you’re using it for the latter purpose,&nbsp;
using filters will save you a lot of time and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.840 --> 00:12:39.280
effort, and also show you more cards. EDHRec&nbsp;
pages will typically show about 300 cards, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.280 --> 00:12:43.840
that’s a decent amount if most of the cards are&nbsp;
ones you’d actually consider, but if two-thirds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.840 --> 00:12:48.000
of them are for disparate archetypes that you&nbsp;
didn’t put in the effort to filter out, suddenly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:54.080
you’re getting a lot less useful information out&nbsp;
of the page. So, uhh, use filters. They’re good.

00:12:54.080 --> 00:12:58.800
As a final note, using EDHREC in general&nbsp;
will likely help your deck out in some form,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.800 --> 00:13:03.200
but I’ve found that getting the best usage out&nbsp;
of it requires some level of detachment from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.200 --> 00:13:07.760
the website. Using other resources and giving&nbsp;
yourself a chance to feel out where your deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.760 --> 00:13:12.880
wants to go will help in giving ideas room to&nbsp;
breathe away from the constant input of how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.880 --> 00:13:18.640
popular cards are. When you are using EDHREC,&nbsp;
it’s useful to sometimes take a step back and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.640 --> 00:13:22.480
remember what the information on your screen&nbsp;
is telling you. The stuff at the top of an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:27.520
EDHREC page is simply the stuff that a bunch of&nbsp;
people decided should go in their deck. Different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.520 --> 00:13:31.120
people might be building decks in different&nbsp;
ways and for different reasons than you are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:35.920
so a card being up there isn’t necessarily a&nbsp;
positive or negative statement about it, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.920 --> 00:13:41.680
it is a very informative one. Even just taking a&nbsp;
bit to think to yourself about why a given card is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.680 --> 00:13:46.240
present at a certain position on the page and what&nbsp;
sorts of decks it might be played in is a really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.240 --> 00:13:50.800
helpful exercise that will make you a lot better&nbsp;
at processing the stuff that’s on your screen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.800 --> 00:13:55.520
and better at understanding the EDHREC&nbsp;
ecosystem in general. Any tool will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.520 --> 00:14:00.320
much more useful to you if you can get a sense&nbsp;
of why it’s going exactly what it’s doing.

00:14:00.320 --> 00:14:04.640
Thanks for watching, and thanks to EDHRec for&nbsp;
sponsoring this video. I also want to thank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.640 --> 00:14:08.800
Archidekt for their ongoing support of the&nbsp;
channel, and its integration of EDHREC’s salt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.800 --> 00:14:13.600
scores and user-polled combo database can provide&nbsp;
interesting tools for seeing how other people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.600 --> 00:14:18.240
might see your deck. Additionally, I want to thank&nbsp;
my Patrons and YouTube members for their support,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.240 --> 00:14:23.760
some of whom are on screen now. As a final note,&nbsp;
The Command Zone just put out a super fun and high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.760 --> 00:14:27.840
effort video a couple days ago to go with Edge&nbsp;
of Eternities, and you should definitely check&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.840 --> 00:14:32.640
it out if you haven’t already—a link to that is in&nbsp;
the description. With that said, here’s a preview&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.640 --> 00:14:38.160
of the EDHRec analyzer video, which will be over&nbsp;
